    Demonstrating Resilience in The Wednesday Wars 

\vspace{10pt}

    The 1960s was a period of great cultural change in America, with many new ideas being 

\vspace{10pt}

    formed in the civil rights movement, counterculture movement, and anti-war movement. Gary D. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Schmidt’s historical fiction novel, The Wednesday Wars, follows Holling Hoodhood’s life as a 

\vspace{10pt}

    junior high school student navigating life in the 1960s. The book explores themes of growing up, 

\vspace{10pt}

    friendship, family, and identity. Resilience is shown throughout as the characters learn these 

\vspace{10pt}

    lessons. In the novel, The Wednesday Wars, by Gary. D Schmidt, Mrs. Baker, Mai Thi, and 

\vspace{10pt}

    Holling all demonstrate resilience as they overcome the challenges of the 1960s as well as the 

\vspace{10pt}

    challenges of their personal lives. 

\vspace{10pt}

    One of the characters who shows resilience is Mrs. Baker. After the news of Mrs. Baker’s 

\vspace{10pt}

    husband, Lt. Tybalt Baker, reports that he is missing in Vietnam, the entire town was anxiously 

\vspace{10pt}

    watching for him in the news. Holling comments on Mrs. Baker’s reaction, remarking, “But you 

\vspace{10pt}

    would never have been able to tell that she was holding her breath at Camillo Junior High. She 

\vspace{10pt}

    moved through our classroom as coolly as if Khesanh were just a proper noun in a sentence that 

\vspace{10pt}

    needed to be diagrammed” (Schimdt 157). Even though Mrs. Baker is going through a lot in her 

\vspace{10pt}

    personal life, she still maintains a calm and collected front with her students. This is because as a 

\vspace{10pt}

    teacher, she puts her students’ needs before her own. She understands that the children need a 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    stable, confident adult amidst the scary things happening in the world. As a result, Mrs. Baker is 

\vspace{10pt}

    a great example of resilience in the novel. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Mai Thi also demonstrates resilience throughout the novel. Mai Thi is Holling’s 

\vspace{10pt}

    classmate who fled the Vietnam War in the summer before seventh grade. The school cook, Mrs. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Bigio, is still affected by her husband’s death in the Vietnam War and blames her feelings onto 

\vspace{10pt}

    Mai Thi. The week before December break, all the students each get a special “Something” as 

\vspace{10pt}

    their treat. From Holling’s perspective, he describes, “Mai Thi took her Something. She looked 

\vspace{10pt}

    down, and kept going. She probably didn’t see that Mrs. Bigio was pulling her hair net down 

\vspace{10pt}

    lower over her face, because she was almost crying. And probably Mrs. Bigio didn’t see that Mai 

\vspace{10pt}

    Thi was almost crying, too” (Schmidt 113). Mai Thi is a young girl who is adjusting to a 

\vspace{10pt}

    completely new life. She faces challenges such as the language barrier, singled out by some 

\vspace{10pt}

    teachers, in addition to the trauma she must have experienced during the war. Mai Thi has to deal 

\vspace{10pt}

    with all of these problems on her own, silently moving forward. She never shows how 

\vspace{10pt}

    overwhelmed she may be. This shows that Mai Thi is an incredibly resilient character. 

\vspace{10pt}

    Furthermore, Holling learns to be resilient as the story progresses. Near the end of the 

\vspace{10pt}

    novel, his sister makes the rash decision to run away with her boyfriend to California in order to 

\vspace{10pt}

    “find herself.” On the journey, however, she realizes that it was a mistake and tries to return 

\vspace{10pt}

    home. When his sister is in dire need of financial support on her way home, Holling is the only 

\vspace{10pt}

    person in their family who helps her. Holling states, “I was there when the bus from Chicago 

\vspace{10pt}

    pulled in at 10:50. The Port Authority was all noise and rushing. The accumulated combustion 

\vspace{10pt}

    from the buses had thickened the air. The whoosh and squeak and hollering of the brakes and the 

\vspace{10pt}

    distorted announcements over the P.A. system and the newsboys hawking and the pell-mell of 

\vspace{10pt}

    more bodies than belong in any one building gave the place a general roar. As for the floor, you 

\vspace{10pt}

\par

\vspace{10pt}

    couldn’t have found a greater confusion if the ceiling had been lifted off and the sky had rained 

\vspace{10pt}

    down ticket stubs and newspapers and Baby Ruth wrappers” (Schimdt 232). Driven by his deep 

\vspace{10pt}

    care for his sister, Holling navigates his way to New York City to visit his sister. The fact that he 

\vspace{10pt}

    is only a junior high school student doesn’t stop him. He lacked money and transportation, but 

\vspace{10pt}

    was determined to find every possible solution to make it work. Holling demonstrates great 

\vspace{10pt}

    resilience in the story. 

\vspace{10pt}

    In the novel The Wednesday Wars, Mrs. Baker, Mai Thi, and Holling all show resilience 

\vspace{10pt}

    as they face various challenges in their lives during the 1960s. Mrs. Baker grapples with 

\vspace{10pt}

    uncertainty for her deployed husband in the Vietnam War, while Mai Thi is constantly 

\vspace{10pt}

    unwelcomed in America. The main character, Holling, shows resilience in his compassion for his 

\vspace{10pt}

    sister. All the characters grow and change to become a better version of themselves as they 

\vspace{10pt}

    resiliently conquer their problems, reflecting the culture of the 1960s. 

\vspace{10pt}

